    {
      "question": "e-7703",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "326aec18bc000715b39cd82104490797b8a6f33c9d0cfaf300257bfa0368d20c",
      "new_sha256": "44240c9cfd11334cdf43d3f01976f2e2318fc801341daba305c9028ca1201c5e",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-7703",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061[{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}\u20613-\\operatorname{ln}\u20614) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}\u20612.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20613-\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/example-7703.json",
